WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.980
Thanks for tuning in to Planetarian Daily, episode 70,

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.400
it's May 26th, 2026.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.020
Here's your science and health briefing.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.340
Satellites have measured sulfur dioxide released

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.280
by refinery fires in Tehran.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.980
USC researchers pinpointed the enzyme CPLA-2

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.040
as a driver of brain inflammation

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.280
in people carrying the high-risk APOE-4 gene.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.100
The enzyme fuels harmful inflammatory responses

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.060
while remaining necessary for normal brain function.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.940
Potential drug compounds were identified

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.540
that reduced this inflammation

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.420
without fully blocking the enzyme.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.440
The approach focuses on modulating,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.600
rather than eliminating CPLA-2 activity.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:44.960
Clinical testing of the compounds

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.700
is the reported next phase.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.360
From brain inflammation pathways

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.380
to atmospheric patterns that also shape health risks,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.140
we turned to a distinct type of heat event

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.760
now better understood through long-term data.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.580
Most people picture heat waves as dry,

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.920
cloudless stretches of scorching air

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.700
that bake the ground and spike dehydration risk.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.140
In reality, a distinct category called moist heat waves

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.060
arrives with tropical cyclones,

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:15.440
bringing high humidity and heavy rain alongside the heat.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:19.180
Right now, atmospheric patterns over the western Pacific

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:22.420
are already capable of steering these systems toward land,

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.800
where the combination of heat and moisture

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.140
stresses both the body's cooling mechanisms

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.220
and infrastructure.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.360
These events have risen to roughly one quarter

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:34.760
of all heat wave days in Japan over the past three decades.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.520
The added humidity prevents sweat

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.020
from evaporating efficiently,

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.940
raising heat stress risk even when air temperatures

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.280
are not at record highs.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.780
One practical step is to watch local forecasts

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.960
for cyclone-linked humidity spikes

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.040
and prepare cooling and hydration plans earlier

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.800
than for classic dry heat waves.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.700
While atmospheric monitoring improves,

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.200
orbital sensors are also proving useful

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.780
for tracking sudden pollution events on the ground.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.320
A research team used a constellation of satellites

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.500
to investigate and quantify sulfur dioxide pollution

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.400
after explosions and fires at oil storage

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.820
and refining facilities in Tehran on March 7th, 2026.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.880
The analysis focused on emissions from multiple sites

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:21.280
damaged in the incident.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.420
Satellite observations allowed direct measurement

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:25.760
of the atmospheric release

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.680
without relying on ground reports.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.500
The work demonstrates how orbital sensors can document

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.860
pollution events in regions with limited on-site access.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.380
Follow-up studies will likely compare these readings

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:41.460
against models of dispersion and health impacts.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.400
From large-scale environmental sensing,

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:46.320
we shift to the opposite extreme.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.000
A chip-scale device that handles light

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.660
for next-generation computing.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:55.000
Researchers at Monash University created a nanoscale circuit

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.180
that generates directs and reads light-based information

00:02:58.180 --> 00:02:59.220
on a single chip.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.440
The device integrates photon handling functions

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:04.840
that previously required separate components.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.040
This integration reduces size and complexity

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.000
for systems that rely on light for data processing.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.760
The circuit targets applications in quantum computing

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.640
and artificial intelligence hardware.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.780
Next steps include scaling the design for broader testing

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:23.080
in integrated photonic platforms.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:26.040
Miniaturized light control on Earth

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:29.320
has a distant counterpart in the early solar system's

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:30.540
planetesimal factories.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.740
Scientists identified a dust-filled ring outside Jupiter

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.100
that functioned as a planetesimal factory

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.140
during the early solar system.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.740
The ring produced multiple generations of space rocks

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:44.260
with distinct compositions.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.160
This mechanism accounts for the varied meteorite types

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:49.540
preserved from that era.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:53.100
The finding resolves questions about how certain primitive

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.900
materials formed and survived.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.800
Further modeling will examine how similar rings operated

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.200
around other giant planets.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:04.520
From solar system formation, we move to the even larger question

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.820
of how magnetic fields arise across cosmic scales.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:11.320
Researchers ran advanced plasma simulations

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:14.880
to show how turbulence generates large-scale magnetic fields

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:15.720
in the universe.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:19.660
The work explains field creation in stars, black holes,

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:22.480
neutron star mergers, and solar eruptions.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.700
Turbulent motions stretch and organize magnetic field lines

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.240
over vast distances.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:31.560
The simulations match observed field strengths

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.200
in different cosmic environments.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:36.020
Future runs will test predictions against

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:38.200
new telescope data on solar activity.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.600
From cosmic magnetism, we return to terrestrial biology

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:44.840
and a newly mapped marine data set.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:49.100
A study compiled 40 years of previously unpublished records

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.580
on marine amphipods from Italian waters.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:54.620
These small crustaceans play key roles

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:55.880
in marine ecosystems.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:58.880
The data set shows greater species variety

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.160
than earlier published accounts indicated.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.560
Researchers digitized and shared the full collection

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:05.620
for broader use.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:08.220
Similar efforts are underway for other

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:09.800
under-reported marine groups.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:11.920
From head and marine biodiversity,

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.460
we look at disease pressures on another under monitored group.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:20.000
A study examined disease susceptibility across snake species

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.500
and found rattlesnakes particularly affected

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:25.640
by ophidiumicosis and parasitic lung infections.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.080
These conditions contribute to population declines

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:30.660
alongside habitat loss.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:34.580
The fungus, ophidiumicis ophidiaicola,

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:38.680
has spread to many species since its discovery two decades ago.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:41.820
Data highlight the need for targeted monitoring

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:43.240
in rattlesnake habitats.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:47.480
Conservation plans will incorporate disease screening protocols.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:49.940
From wildlife health monitoring,

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.120
we turn to an unexpected archeological clue

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:54.760
about early pharmacology.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:58.220
Microscopic residues on scissors and tweezers

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:01.640
from a 1348 to 1411 CE TUM

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.800
in Dyeong-Yin, China show controlled use

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.060
of a toxic chemical as an anesthetic.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.500
The evidence points to sophisticated

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:12.320
pharmacological knowledge during the Ming period.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:15.380
The substance was applied in measured amounts

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:16.360
during procedures.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.380
This marks the earliest documented instance

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:22.040
of such targeted anesthetic use in the region.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:26.060
Further chemical analysis will identify the exact compound.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.560
From historical pharmacology,

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:31.420
we close with an observational longevity signal

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:34.040
that warrants further metabolic study.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:37.740
Observational data connect a common diabetes medication

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:40.520
to longer life spans among women who took it.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.120
The association appears in records tracking health outcomes

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:44.920
over time.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:47.900
Researchers note the finding adds to evidence

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:50.520
on metabolic pathways that influence aging.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.540
The study does not establish causation

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:56.380
but identifies a signal for further investigation.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:00.720
Additional analyses will examine dose and duration effects.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:03.820
Next time, keep an eye on follow-up work

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:06.240
comparing satellite sulfur dioxide readings

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:07.480
to dispersion models.

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:10.320
That covers today's science and health news.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:12.520
Share this with someone who's curious

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:13.840
about the latest research.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:15.540
I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:16.740
See you next time.

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:20.920
This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice.

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:23.780
Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
